<h2>4. Questions and Answers</h2>
<h3>Q1. Why do we use the EM algorithm to estimate machine yield rate?</h3><p>The machine yield rates are not directly observable because a final defect can be caused by one of several machines used earlier in the process. EM handles this hidden-cause problem: the E-step estimates how many observed bad pieces should be assigned to each possible machine, and the M-step updates each machine yield rate from those expected bad and good counts.</p><h3>Q2. After the manufacturer has the machine yield-rate estimation results, what can they do?</h3><p>The manufacturer can rank machines by estimated yield rate and expected bad pieces, identify the machines most likely to create defects, prioritize maintenance or calibration, schedule inspection for high-risk stations, and compare machines in the same process stage.</p><h3>Q3. What did I learn from this lab?</h3><p>I learned how an EM workflow converts incomplete manufacturing observations into usable machine-level estimates. I also learned that preprocessing, including lot grouping, one-hot machine encoding, lot yield calculation, and bad-piece handling, strongly affects the final ranking.</p><h3>Q4. Any questions and/or difficulties in following the EM Algorithm topic?</h3><p>The most difficult part is understanding the E-step allocation: one observed defect is distributed across several possible machines according to current yield estimates and process order. The real dataset also takes much longer to run than the simplified case.</p>

<h2>5. Conclusion</h2>
<p>For the simplified dataset, MS-14 has the lowest estimated yield rate. For the real-world dataset, LS-061 has the lowest estimated yield rate, followed by PL-021 and LS-070. These machines should be reviewed first because the EM results indicate they are the most likely contributors to observed defects.</p>
